Relational Meme Fitness:

A Measurement and Identification Framework for Content, Response, and Algorithmic Distribution

Version 0.2 — Theoretical Preregistration

---

Abstract

We propose a theoretical and methodological framework for studying the propagation of textual content in social environments governed by algorithmic recommendation. Our central contribution is to reformulate meme fitness not as an intrinsic property of a text, but as a relational property emerging from the coupling between a linguistic structure Z, a human behavioral response M, a proxy for algorithmic distribution Q, and a proxy for audience composition \widetilde A.

We introduce an explicit bilinear form for fitness,

F_{it}=\left\langle\phi(Z_{it}),\Psi(\widetilde A_{it},R_{it},G_{it})\right\rangle,

which renders the relational claim mathematically testable and distinguishes theoretical fitness — a latent construct — from its observable manifestations M,Q,\widetilde A.

This paper is the first component of a two-stage research program. It tests the upstream segment of the proposed Recursive Mirror mechanism, namely the temporally ordered chain

Z_{i,t-1}\rightarrow M_{i,t}\rightarrow Q_{i,t+1}\rightarrow\widetilde A_{i,t+1}.

The full loop \widetilde A_{i,t+1}\rightarrow Z_{i,t+2}, which constitutes the distinctive signature of the Recursive Mirror, is explicitly deferred to a second longitudinal study and is not identified here.

We preregister measurements, reference corpora, identification strategy, falsification tests, and analysis plan prior to observing any performance data. LLMs are treated as measurement instruments, never as ground truth. Six falsification scenarios are specified.

Keywords: meme fitness, recommendation systems, linguistic measurement, causal identification, preregistration, LLM-as-instrument.

---

1. Introduction

1.1 The Problem

Since Dawkins' (1976) foundational work, the notion of a meme has designated a unit of cultural transmission subject to selection pressures analogous to those governing biological evolution. Applied to digital content, this analogy has generated a prolific vocabulary — virality, propagation, fitness — but little testable formalization.

The difficulty is twofold.

First, the fitness of a content is almost always defined by its observed success. A text is said to be fit because it propagated; its propagation is then explained by its fitness. The circularity is patent.

Second, contemporary propagation environments are algorithmically mediated. On platforms such as X, TikTok, YouTube, or Facebook, the distribution of a content is not a simple effect of its perceived quality within a social network, but the product of a coupling between human cognition, graph structure, and proprietary recommendation systems. Any theory of meme fitness that ignores this coupling measures at best a correlation between text and raw performance.

1.2 The Thesis

We defend the following thesis:

The meme fitness of a content is not an intrinsic property of the text. It is a relational property of the coupling between textual structure, human cognition, social structure, and algorithmic distribution.

Formally, this implies that fitness is not a function of Z alone, but a scalar product between an encoding of the text and an encoding of the environment:

F_{it}=\left\langle\phi(Z_{it}),\Psi(\widetilde A_{it},R_{it},G_{it})\right\rangle.

This formulation has three immediate theoretical consequences:

1. The same text has different fitness values in different environments. This is not a metaphor: it is a testable prediction on cross-posted content.
2. Fitness is not directly observable. F is a latent construct, distinct from its manifestations M,Q,\widetilde A.
3. Fitness is not stable over time. The environment evolves; therefore fitness does too.

1.3 A Two-Stage Research Program

The complete program, which we call the Recursive Mirror, postulates the loop

Z_{t}\rightarrow M_{t}\rightarrow Q_{t+1}\rightarrow\widetilde A_{t+1}\rightarrow Z_{t+2}.

This loop asserts that the propagation of a content progressively transforms the audience which, in turn, transforms the author's future production. Content modifies the environment that selects it.

We do not claim to identify this complete loop in the present article. Such an objective would require:

· a longitudinal panel of 12 to 24 months;
· a quasi-experimental design allowing the separation of audience mutation from raw performance;
· repeated measurement of Z and \widetilde A for the same authors.

What we do here is more modest, and more honest: we build the instrument capable of putting this theory at risk. We test the upstream segment of the proposed causal chain, and we specify explicitly the conditions under which the theory would be falsified.

1.4 What This Article Is Not

This article is:

· not an attempt at reverse-engineering the X algorithm;
· not a demonstration of the complete Recursive Mirror loop;
· not a claim of causality in the strict experimental sense;
· not an authorization to optimize informational manipulation.

It is a methodological contribution: a preregistered measurement framework for testing a relational theory of meme fitness within a real algorithmic ecology.

