2. Formal Theory

2.1 The Space of Textual Structures

Let a content be indexed by (i,t), where i denotes the author and t the temporal position. We define the linguistic vector

\mathbf Z_{it}=(D_{it},S_{it},X_{it},K_{it},R_{it})\in\mathbb R^5,

where:

· D_{it}: conceptual density;
· S_{it}: associative surprise;
· X_{it}: cross-register collision;
· K_{it}: cognitive acquisition cost;
· R_{it}: contextual resonance.

Conceptual density. Measures the quantity of conceptual structure per unit of text:

D=\frac{|U_{\text{concepts}}|}{\text{tokens}}.

Associative surprise. Measures the improbability of a conceptual association under a reference distribution P:

S_{P}(c_1,c_2)=-\log\frac{P(c_1,c_2)}{P(c_1)P(c_2)}.

This quantity corresponds to a negative pointwise mutual information (PMI). We handle it with smoothing, a minimum frequency threshold, and normalization by concept rarity. Three variants are defined,

S_G,\quad S_T,\quad S_X,

corresponding to the three reference corpora introduced in Section 3.

Cross-register collision. Measures the distance between discursive registers simultaneously mobilized:

X=\frac{1}{n(n-1)}\sum_{i\neq j}d(r_i,r_j),

where the r_i belong to a preregistered taxonomy

\mathcal R=\{\text{scientific},\text{technical},\text{philosophical},\text{political},\text{religious},\text{humorous},\text{institutional},\text{intimate},\text{internet},\ldots\}.

Cognitive cost. A composite:

K=z(L)+z(P)+z(A)-z(F),

where L denotes low readability, P syntactic depth, A referential ambiguity, and F conceptual familiarity. We preregister a factor analysis: if these components do not form a coherent factor, K is not treated as unidimensional.

Contextual resonance. Captures the correspondence between the content and current events, a controversy, or an activated cultural imaginary. Operationalized as the similarity between the tweet embedding and the embedding of a contemporary news stream.

2.2 Environment and Relational Fitness

Let an environment be \mathcal E_{it}=(\widetilde A_{it},R_{it},G_{it}), where:

· \widetilde A_{it}: observable reactive audience;
· R_{it}: contextual resonance;
· G_{it}: local structure of the author's social graph.

We define two encodings:

\phi:\text{text}\to\mathbb R^d,\qquad \Psi:\text{environment}\to\mathbb R^d.

Fitness is then

\boxed{F_{it}=\left\langle\phi(Z_{it}),\Psi(\mathcal E_{it})\right\rangle.}

Fundamental distinction. F is a latent theoretical construct. It is not observed. What is observed is:

· the behavioral response M;
· the distribution proxy Q;
· the audience proxy \widetilde A.

Conflating F with any of its manifestations would render the theory tautological. We do not do so.

2.3 Derived Predictions

The bilinear form yields three distinct and testable predictions.

Prediction 1 (cross-post). For the same Z exposed to two environments \mathcal E_1\neq\mathcal E_2:

F(Z,\mathcal E_1)\neq F(Z,\mathcal E_2).

Prediction 2 (temporal reversal). A content successful at t may fail at t+k, even if identical, if \mathcal E has mutated.

Prediction 3 (loop). If the Recursive Mirror operates, then

\left\langle\phi(Z_{t+2}),\Psi(\mathcal E_{t+1})\right\rangle>\left\langle\phi(Z_t),\Psi(\mathcal E_t)\right\rangle.

This last prediction belongs to the longitudinal component and is not tested here.

2.4 Cognitive Envelope and Conditional Threshold

We distinguish K, a continuous cost, from a binary incomprehensibility indicator:

I(Z)=\mathbf 1\{K(Z)>\kappa(S(Z))\}.

The function \kappa is initially assumed affine,

\kappa(S)=a+bS,

then tested in spline form. This separation avoids the redundancy between falsifications F2 and F3 (Section 6).

The underlying hypothesis is that surprise is beneficial only within a cognitive accessibility envelope:

\frac{\partial P}{\partial S}>0\quad\text{for }K<\kappa(S),\qquad
\frac{\partial P}{\partial K}<0\quad\text{for }K>\kappa(S).

These are predictions, not axioms.

---

3. Measurement Model

3.1 Status of the LLM

The protocol is categorical:

\boxed{\text{LLM coding}\neq\text{ground truth}.}

LLMs are instruments. Several blind judges are used, with frozen prompts and preregistered criteria. The LLM never receives performance information:

\text{LLM}_{\text{coding}}\not\leftarrow\{\text{likes},\text{replies},\text{quotes},\text{views}\}.

This strict separation prevents contamination of the explanatory variable by the dependent variable.

3.2 Triangulation

Each tweet is coded along three independent levels:

Variable Human annotation Blind LLM Computational geometry
D conceptual units structured extraction semantic dispersion/clustering
S unpredictability criterion score associative surprisal
X registers classification cross-register distance
K perceived difficulty readability score length + complexity + semantic measures

3.3 Gold Standard and Reliability

A subsample of 500 tweets receives expert human coding (at least three annotators). We report:

· human inter-rater agreement via Krippendorff's \alpha;
· LLM–human concordance;
· computational measure–human concordance.

If methods diverge strongly, we do not force their convergence. Divergence becomes a methodological result in its own right.

---

4. Temporal Causal Graph

4.1 Canonical Architecture

\boxed{
Z_{i,t-1}\rightarrow M_{i,t}\rightarrow Q_{i,t+1}\rightarrow\widetilde A_{i,t+1}.
}

Each arrow is tested separately.

4.2 Operational Definitions

M_{i,t} — behavioral response:

M_{it}=\begin{bmatrix}\text{Quote/Reply}\\\text{Dwell Time}\\\text{Bookmark}\\\text{Reply Depth}\\\text{Conversation Length}\end{bmatrix}.

Q_{i,t+1} — proxy for algorithmic distribution. Never identified with the algorithm itself:

Q=\text{impressions/exposures, new engagers, non-follower share, diffusion depth, community diversity reached}.

\widetilde A_{i,t+1} — proxy for observable reactive audience, not total audience:

P(\widetilde A_t)=\text{community distribution among engagers}.

4.3 Explicit Confounders

The causal graph is credible only if confounders are named. We identify four families:

1. Author-invariant factors (reputation, initial network, style) — absorbed by fixed effects \alpha_i.
2. Global temporal factors (news, seasonality, platform events) — absorbed by fixed effects \lambda_t.
3. Author-time variable factors (mood, production cycle, thematic load) — partially controlled by covariates X_{it}.
4. Selection on exposure — Q is observed only conditional on a minimum exposure (Section 7).

4.4 Preregistered Models

Mediation equation:

M_{it}=\alpha_i+\beta^\top Z_{i,t-1}+\gamma R_{i,t-1}+u_{it}.

Distribution equation:

Q_{i,t+1}=\alpha_i+\delta M_{it}+\theta^\top Z_{i,t-1}+\lambda_t+\phi X_{it}+v_{i,t+1}.

Audience equation:

\Delta\widetilde A_{i,t+1}=\alpha_i+\eta Q_{i,t+1}+\psi M_{it}+\omega^\top Z_{i,t-1}+\lambda_t+\epsilon_{i,t+1}.

The fixed effects \alpha_i absorb time-invariant author heterogeneity. \lambda_t absorb common temporal shocks.

---

5. Identification Strategy

5.1 Epistemic Positioning

We explicitly adopt the following stance:

We estimate temporally ordered associations and test predictions derived from a causal model. Absent an identification design, we do not interpret observational estimates as causal effects.

This formulation protects against two symmetric pitfalls: naive positivism (interpreting any temporal correlation as causality) and paralyzing skepticism (refusing all inference).

5.2 Within-Author Fixed Effects

The main strategy is

Y_{it}=\alpha_i+\beta^\top Z_{i,t-1}+\lambda_t+\epsilon_{it},

complemented by a matched control corpus on

\text{followers},\ \text{age},\ \text{frequency},\ \text{topic},\ \text{language},\ \text{network}.

5.3 Secondary Quasi-Experimental Design

Four candidate designs, preregistered in order of preference:

1. Exogenous platform policy change. Difference-in-differences:
   Y_{it}=\alpha_i+\lambda_t+\beta(\text{Post}_t\times\text{Exposed}_i)+\gamma X_{it}+\epsilon_{it},
   with parallel-trends verification before any interpretation.
2. Cross-community cascade. If a tweet is picked up by a community absent from \widetilde A_t, one obtains a quasi-exogenous \Delta\widetilde A.
3. Author thematic pivot. Regression discontinuity at the point of topic change.
4. Detectable distribution shock. Sensitivity analysis only — shadowban detection is methodologically fragile and cannot serve as a primary instrument.

5.4 Causal Caution

We systematically avoid the formulation "M causes Y." We speak of temporal mediation compatible with the causal hypothesis. The linguistic difference is small, but epistemically essential.

---

6. Falsification

Six preregistered scenarios.

F1 — Density without propagation:

D\uparrow,\quad Y\nrightarrow\uparrow.

→ Conceptual density is not sufficient.

F2 — Excessive cognitive cost:

K\uparrow\ \text{with}\ K>\kappa(S),\quad Y\downarrow.

→ Cost exceeds the accessibility envelope.

F3 — Collision turned into noise:

X\uparrow,\quad I(Z)=1,\quad Y\downarrow.

→ Collision crosses the incomprehensibility threshold.

F4 — Response without redistribution:

M\uparrow,\quad Q\nrightarrow\uparrow.

→ Behavioral mediation does not translate into distribution.

F5 — Redistribution without audience mutation:

Q\uparrow,\quad JSD(P(\widetilde A_t),P(\widetilde A_{t+1}))\approx 0.

→ Amplification without audience transformation.

F6 — Mutation without textual cause:

JSD(P(\widetilde A_t),P(\widetilde A_{t+1}))\uparrow

without prior modification of Z.
→ Mutation originates from other network mechanisms.

These scenarios are not failures of the program: they are its possible outcomes. A theory that cannot fail cannot be tested.

---

7. Selection on Exposure

Q is observed only conditional on a minimum exposure. Two tweets with the same Z may differ systematically in their probability of being exposed. This produces endogenous selection.

Strategy:

1. Selection model (Heckman). Selection equation on exposure, then equation of interest conditional on selection, with control term \lambda(\cdot).
2. Restriction to minimum-exposure tweets. Sensitivity analysis: do results hold when restricting to tweets that received at least one engager?
3. Manski bounds. If selection is non-ignorable, we report bounds rather than points.

None of these strategies fully resolves the problem. They allow it to be characterized.

---

8. Pilot Study

Platform: one.

Language: one.

Window: 3–6 months.

N: 10,000–50,000 tweets.

Human coding: ~500 tweets.

Three reference corpora:

· \mathcal C_G — general reference (Wikipedia + encyclopedic and journalistic corpora);
· \mathcal C_T — temporal reference (contemporary corpus of the window);
· \mathcal C_X — platform-native reference (public corpus of comparable messages, same platform, same window).

Strict temporal separation:

\mathcal C^{\text{baseline}}_{t-1}\cap\mathcal C^{\text{test}}_{t}=\varnothing.

Surprise thereby becomes predictive rather than retrospective.

Primary analysis: estimation of Z_{t-1}\to M_t\to Q_{t+1}\to\Delta\widetilde A_{t+1} with within-author fixed effects, temporal fixed effects, and author-time controls.

Secondary analyses: quasi-experimental design if an exogenous event is available; selection sensitivity.

Preregistration: hypotheses, measurements, thresholds, falsifications, and analysis plan frozen before observation.

---

9. Expected Results and Analysis Plan

No results are included in this version. The analysis plan is preregistered:

1. Descriptive statistics of Z,M,Q,\widetilde A.
2. Factor analysis of K (unidimensionality).
3. Main regressions with fixed effects.
4. Mediation tests (Sobel, bootstrap).
5. Selection analysis (Heckman, bounds).
6. Falsifications F1–F6 with preregistered thresholds.
7. Robustness analyses: sensitivity to alternative measures of S, X, K.
8. Methodological divergences: LLM vs. humans vs. computational.

---

10. Limitations

Limitation 1. The full loop \widetilde A\to Z' is not identified. The Recursive Mirror remains a programmatic hypothesis.

Limitation 2. The proxies Q and \widetilde A are neither the algorithm nor the total audience. Any inference remains relative to these observables.

Limitation 3. The observational design does not guarantee the absence of unobserved confounding. The estimates are compatible with the causal model; they do not demonstrate it.

Limitation 4. LLM coding introduces an instrument bias. Triangulation characterizes it; it does not eliminate it.

Limitation 5. A single platform, a single language, a single window: external generalization is limited by construction.

Limitation 6. Fitness F remains a latent construct, not directly measured.

---

11. Ethics and Dual Use

The proposed framework describes a propagation mechanism. If this mechanism is real, its description is also a potential specification for the optimization of informational manipulation.

We distinguish two levels:

· Explanation: understanding how textual structures interact with algorithmic environments to produce propagation dynamics.
· Optimization: providing operational recipes for targeting or manipulating populations.

The present article belongs to the first level. It provides:

· no objective function to maximize;
· no optimization procedure;
· no method for individual targeting.

We insist on one point: transparency is here preferable to secrecy. A theory of algorithmic propagation that remains implicit within platform teams alone constitutes a democratic risk. A theory made public, testable, and debated by a scientific community becomes an object of discussion and regulation.

---

12. Longitudinal Extension: The Recursive Mirror

12.1 The Complete Loop

\boxed{
Z_{t}\rightarrow M_{t}\rightarrow Q_{t+1}\rightarrow\widetilde A_{t+1}\rightarrow Z_{t+2}.
}

This loop is the proper object of the second component of the program.

12.2 The Decisive Test

Two groups of authors:

G_1:\Delta Q>0,\ \Delta\widetilde A\approx 0,

G_2:\Delta Q>0,\ \Delta\widetilde A>0.

Prediction:

E[\Delta Z'\,|\,G_2]\ >\ E[\Delta Z'\,|\,G_1].

Both groups experienced an increase in distribution Q. They differ in audience mutation \widetilde A.

If the prediction holds, one can distinguish:

\text{Virality}\neq\text{Recursive Mirror}.

The former is explicable by Q alone. The latter requires an additional contribution of \widetilde A to Z'.

12.3 Identification Condition

The mutation \widetilde A must be quasi-exogenous with respect to Z'. Without this, one cannot distinguish:

· the effect of audience mutation on future production;
· the effect of confounding factors (news, time availability, life changes).

Three candidate designs for the second component:

1. Cross-community cascade with measurable \Delta\widetilde A.
2. Platform policy change affecting audience composition without changing the author's production.
3. Thematic pivot of an author followed longitudinally.

12.4 Required Panel

12–24 months. Repeated measures of Z and \widetilde A for the same sample of authors. Ideally, several platforms to distinguish platform effects from general effects.

---

13. Conclusion

We have proposed a theoretical and methodological framework for studying meme fitness as a relational property. This framework:

· distinguishes theoretical fitness F from its observable manifestations M,Q,\widetilde A;
· formalizes fitness as a bilinear form between textual and environmental encodings;
· strictly separates preregistered measurements from exploratory analyses;
· treats the LLM as an instrument, not as truth;
· specifies six falsification scenarios;
· explicitly acknowledges the limits of observational identification.

The present article tests the upstream segment Z\to M\to Q\to\widetilde A of the proposed loop. The complete Recursive Mirror loop — \widetilde A\to Z' — remains a programmatic hypothesis, to be identified in a second longitudinal component.

The thesis we defend is simple:

\boxed{\text{Meme Fitness is Relational.}}

A memetic object does not have a fixed fitness. It has a fitness within a given environment, at a given moment. And in a coupled author–text–audience–algorithm system, that environment is itself transformed by propagation.

Understanding this loop — rather than attempting to reconstruct the proprietary code of a platform — constitutes the scientific object of our program.

