The Recursive Mirror:

A Longitudinal Framework for Audience-Driven Production in Algorithmic Environments

Version 0.3 — Theoretical Preregistration, Second Component

---

Abstract

This paper constitutes the second component of a two-stage research program on relational meme fitness. The first component (v0.2) established a measurement and identification framework for the upstream segment of the proposed Recursive Mirror mechanism, namely the temporally ordered chain

Z_{i,t-1}\rightarrow M_{i,t}\rightarrow Q_{i,t+1}\rightarrow\widetilde A_{i,t+1}.

The present paper addresses the downstream segment:

\widetilde A_{i,t+1}\rightarrow Z_{i,t+2}.

The central theoretical claim is that content propagation does not merely amplify a text; it transforms the reactive audience, and this transformation subsequently modifies the author's future production. We call this the Recursive Mirror: the content transforms the audience that transforms the content.

We propose a longitudinal panel design over 12 to 24 months, with repeated measurement of linguistic structure Z and reactive audience \widetilde A for the same authors. The central test compares two groups of authors:

G_1:\Delta Q>0,\ \Delta\widetilde A\approx 0,

G_2:\Delta Q>0,\ \Delta\widetilde A>0,

where Q is a proxy for algorithmic distribution. Both groups experienced increased distribution. They differ in whether that distribution was accompanied by audience mutation. The Recursive Mirror predicts

E[\Delta Z'\,|\,G_2]\ >\ E[\Delta Z'\,|\,G_1].

If confirmed, this distinguishes virality (replication driven by distribution) from recursive mirroring (production driven by audience transformation). Three quasi-experimental designs are preregistered for identifying exogenous audience mutation. Six falsification scenarios are specified.

Keywords: recursive mirror, audience mutation, longitudinal panel, meme fitness, algorithmic feedback, causal identification.

---

1. Introduction

1.1 From Upstream to Loop

The first component of this program (v0.2) tested a temporally ordered chain from textual structure to behavioral response to distribution to audience. That chain is directional but not circular. It establishes that certain linguistic configurations produce distinctive responses, and that these responses are associated with increased distribution, and that this distribution is compatible with a measurable shift in reactive audience composition.

What it does not establish is the return: whether the audience shift feeds back into the author's future production.

This is the theoretical core of the Recursive Mirror. Without the return, the framework reduces to a sophisticated theory of virality — content performs, content is amplified, authors produce more content. With the return, the framework becomes a theory of co-evolution: content transforms the cognitive and social milieu within which future content is produced.

1.2 The Claim

We defend the following claim:

In algorithmically mediated environments, propagation is not only a selection mechanism operating on content. It is a transformation mechanism operating on the audience, and through the audience, on the producer.

Formally, the loop is

\boxed{
Z_{t}\rightarrow M_{t}\rightarrow Q_{t+1}\rightarrow\widetilde A_{t+1}\rightarrow Z_{t+2}.
}

The distinctive theoretical content is in the last arrow. The first three are propagation. The last is recursion.

1.3 Why This Is Not Virality

Virality theory says: content that performs is replicated. The mechanism is selection — good content spreads, bad content dies.

Recursive Mirror says: content that performs attracts a different audience, and that audience reshapes the production of future content. The mechanism is feedback through audience composition, not through performance directly.

The empirical difference is sharp. Two authors may both experience a surge in distribution Q. In the virality account, both should produce more of the same. In the Recursive Mirror account, only the author whose audience composition shifted should exhibit systematic production change. The other should remain stable.

This is the test we preregister.

1.4 What This Paper Is Not

This paper is:

· not an identification of the algorithmic mechanism itself;
· not a claim that authors consciously adapt to audience shifts;
· not a claim that audience mutation is the only driver of production change;
· not a demonstration that the loop is closed in every case.

It is a longitudinal framework for testing whether the return arrow is empirically detectable under quasi-experimental conditions.

---

2. Theoretical Upgrade from Component One

2.1 What Carries Over

The following elements from v0.2 are retained without modification:

· the linguistic vector Z=(D,S,X,K,R);
· the behavioral response M;
· the distribution proxy Q;
· the audience proxy \widetilde A;
· the bilinear fitness form F=\langle\phi(Z),\Psi(\widetilde A,R,G)\rangle;
· the distinction between latent fitness and observable manifestations;
· the treatment of LLMs as instruments, not truth.

2.2 What Is New

The second component introduces four theoretical objects.

First, a production operator. We posit a function

Z_{t+2}=\Gamma(\widetilde A_{t+1},\mathcal H_t,\Theta_i)+\xi_{t+2},

where \mathcal H_t denotes the author's history and \Theta_i her time-invariant production style.

Second, an adaptation direction. Production change \Delta Z'=Z_{t+2}-Z_t is not random. The Recursive Mirror predicts it aligns with the characteristics of the mutated audience:

\Delta Z'\ \text{aligned with}\ \Psi(\widetilde A_{t+1})-\Psi(\widetilde A_t).

Third, a distinction between performance and audience. The two are empirically correlated but theoretically distinct. The design must separate them.

Fourth, a temporal horizon. Audience mutation is slow. The effect of \widetilde A_{t+1} on Z_{t+2} may require months, not days. The panel must be long enough to observe it.

2.3 The Central Prediction

If the audience mutates from \widetilde A_t to \widetilde A_{t+1}, and if the author adapts to this new audience, then the fitness of future production should increase with respect to the new audience, even if it decreases with respect to the old one:

\langle\phi(Z_{t+2}),\Psi(\widetilde A_{t+1})\rangle > \langle\phi(Z_t),\Psi(\widetilde A_{t+1})\rangle.

This is the internal signature of the loop. It is not equivalent to an increase in raw performance Q. It is an increase in alignment between production and environment.

---

3. The G₁/G₂ Design

3.1 Motivation

The central difficulty of the downstream test is that distribution Q and audience mutation \Delta\widetilde A are correlated. Authors whose content is amplified tend to attract new audiences. If we simply regress \Delta Z' on Q, we cannot distinguish:

· adaptation to performance (behaviorist account);
· adaptation to audience (Recursive Mirror account).

We need a design in which Q increases but \Delta\widetilde A does not, at least in some cases.

3.2 The Two Groups

Define two groups of author-episodes:

G_1:\Delta Q>0,\quad \Delta\widetilde A\approx 0,

G_2:\Delta Q>0,\quad \Delta\widetilde A>0.

Here \Delta Q is the change in distribution proxy over a window t\to t+1, and \Delta\widetilde A is the Jensen–Shannon divergence between the reactive audience at t and at t+1:

\Delta\widetilde A_t=JSD\!\left(P(\widetilde A_t),P(\widetilde A_{t+1})\right).

The threshold for "\approx 0" and ">0" is preregistered before observation.

3.3 The Prediction

\boxed{
E[\Delta Z'\,|\,G_2]\ >\ E[\Delta Z'\,|\,G_1].
}

The dependent variable \Delta Z' is a vector. We test the prediction on each component and on the composite alignment measure introduced in Section 2.3.

3.4 Why This Is the Decisive Test

Both groups experienced increased distribution. They differ in whether that distribution transformed the audience.

· If the Recursive Mirror operates, only G_2 should exhibit systematic production change.
· If only distribution matters, both groups should respond similarly.
· If neither group responds, the downstream arrow is not detectable at the panel horizon.

Each outcome is theoretically informative. The design is constructed so that the null result is as meaningful as the positive one.

3.5 The Comparison with a Third Group

We additionally include a control group:

G_0:\Delta Q\approx 0,\quad \Delta\widetilde A\approx 0.

This group provides a baseline for natural drift in production style. Without it, one cannot distinguish adaptation from ordinary authorial evolution.

The preregistered contrasts are G_2>G_1 and G_2>G_0. The G_1 vs. G_0 contrast tests whether distribution alone — without audience mutation — produces any production change.

---

4. Measuring Production Change \Delta Z'

4.1 The Problem

Production change is a high-dimensional object. Authors change topics, styles, registers, and rhythms simultaneously. A naive comparison of raw text will capture everything, including noise.

We preregister three levels of measurement.

4.2 Level 1 — Component Change

For each component of Z:

\Delta D'=D_{t+2}-D_t,\quad \Delta S'=S_{t+2}-S_t,\quad \Delta X'=X_{t+2}-X_t,\quad \Delta K'=K_{t+2}-K_t.

These are analyzed separately. An increase in D after audience mutation would indicate that the author produces denser content for a new audience. A decrease in K would indicate simplification.

4.3 Level 2 — Alignment Change

\Delta F'=\langle\phi(Z_{t+2}),\Psi(\widetilde A_{t+1})\rangle-\langle\phi(Z_t),\Psi(\widetilde A_{t+1})\rangle.

This measures whether production has moved toward the new audience, holding the audience fixed at its post-mutation state.

The complementary measure,

\Delta F''=\langle\phi(Z_{t+2}),\Psi(\widetilde A_t)\rangle-\langle\phi(Z_t),\Psi(\widetilde A_t)\rangle,

measures whether production has moved away from the old audience. If the Recursive Mirror is operating, we expect \Delta F'>0 and possibly \Delta F''\leq 0.

4.4 Level 3 — Distributional Change

The entire distribution of production is compared:

\Delta P_Z=JSD\!\left(P(Z_{t:t+k}),P(Z_{t+k:t+2k})\right),

where P(Z) denotes the empirical distribution of the author's linguistic vector over a window. This captures changes that are invisible at the level of individual components — for instance, an increase in variance without a change in mean.

4.5 Preregistered Hierarchy

We preregister the following hierarchy of tests:

1. Primary: \Delta F' under G_2 vs. G_1.
2. Secondary: component-wise \Delta Z' under G_2 vs. G_1.
3. Tertiary: distributional change \Delta P_Z under G_2 vs. G_1.

The primary test is protected against multiple comparisons. Secondary and tertiary tests are exploratory with preregistered direction but no alpha correction.

---

5. Identification Strategy

5.1 The Core Problem

The naive comparison G_2 vs. G_1 is confounded by at least four mechanisms:

1. Selection into G_2. Authors whose audience mutates may differ systematically from those whose audience does not.
2. Common cause. Both audience mutation and production change may be driven by an external event (news, personal change, platform shift).
3. Reverse causality. Authors may have already begun producing differently, which attracted a new audience — the arrow runs backward.
4. Anticipation. Authors may anticipate audience shift and preemptively adapt.

Without an identification design, the G_2>G_1 contrast is observational and compatible with all four mechanisms.

5.2 The Preregistered Designs

We preregister three quasi-experimental designs, in order of preference.

5.3 Design A — Exogenous Cross-Community Cascade

Setup. A content produced by author i is picked up by a community C that was previously absent from \widetilde A_i.

Identification. The cascade event is plausibly exogenous to the author's production plan if:

· the cascade originates from a third-party account, not the author;
· the cascade occurs shortly after publication, before the author can respond strategically;
· the community C is identifiable and measurably distinct from the prior audience.

Estimator. Within-author difference-in-differences:

Y_{it}=\alpha_i+\lambda_t+\beta(\text{Post}_t\times\text{Cascade}_i)+\gamma X_{it}+\epsilon_{it}.

The pre-trend check must show parallel production trajectories before the cascade.

Threats. Cascades may be caused by content characteristics that also drive subsequent production. We mitigate by conditioning on Z_{i,t-1} and by restricting to cascades whose timing is plausibly exogenous.

5.4 Design B — Platform Policy Change

Setup. A documented change in platform distribution policy affects the composition of audiences reached by some authors but not others.

Identification. If the policy change is:

· documented and dated;
· orthogonal to author production choices;
· affecting groups that trend in parallel before the change;

then \Delta\widetilde A in the affected group is quasi-exogenous.

Estimator. Difference-in-differences with group and time fixed effects:

Y_{it}=\alpha_i+\lambda_t+\beta(\text{Post}_t\times\text{Affected}_i)+\gamma X_{it}+\epsilon_{it}.

Threats. Policy changes are rarely clean. The affected group may be selected. The pre-trend check is essential and cannot be waived.

5.5 Design C — Thematic Pivot with Audience Displacement

Setup. An author pivots thematically. The pivot is identified ex ante by an external marker: a new project, a new publication, a new collaboration.

Identification. If the pivot is documented and dated, we can measure whether the audience shifted after the pivot and whether production changed further in response to the shift.

Estimator. Regression discontinuity in time at the pivot:

Y_{it}=\alpha_i+f(t-t_0)+\beta\,\mathbf 1\{t>t_0\}+\epsilon_{it},

with f a flexible function of time around the pivot.

Threats. The pivot itself is endogenous to authorial intent. This design is weaker than A or B and is preregistered as tertiary.

5.6 What We Do Not Use

We do not use shadowban detection. The phenomenon is real but methodologically fragile: without an independent, external indication of the ban, a drop in Q is indistinguishable from ordinary variance, and the inferred cause becomes circular.

---

6. Falsification

Six preregistered scenarios.

F1 — No downstream effect.

G_2>G_1\ \text{not observed}.

→ The downstream arrow is not detectable at the panel horizon.

F2 — Distribution alone explains production change.

G_1\approx G_2,\quad G_1>G_0.

→ Adaptation is driven by performance, not audience mutation. Virality account prevails.

F3 — Audience mutation without production change.

\Delta\widetilde A>0,\quad \Delta Z'\approx 0.

→ Audience shifts occur but do not feedback.

F4 — Production change without alignment.

\Delta Z'\neq 0,\quad \Delta F'\approx 0.

→ Production drifts but does not move toward the new audience.

F5 — Alignment moves away from new audience.

\Delta F'<0.

→ The opposite of the Recursive Mirror prediction. Production moves away from the audience that selected it.

F6 — Anticipatory production.

\Delta Z'\ \text{precedes}\ \Delta\widetilde A.

→ The causal direction is reversed.

Each scenario is informative. F1 through F3 weaken the Recursive Mirror. F4 and F5 weaken the alignment interpretation. F6 reverses it.

---

7. Panel Specification

7.1 Dimensions

Duration: 12–24 months.

Authors: 200–500 per platform, selected to ensure coverage across:

· initial audience size;
· thematic domain;
· posting frequency;
· language.

Measurement cadence: Z measured weekly per author; \widetilde A measured monthly; Q measured continuously.

Windows:

· t: reference window (baseline production and audience).
· t+1: mutation window (audience shift).
· t+2: adaptation window (production response).

Lengths of windows are preregistered per platform, but the minimum t\to t+2 span is 6 months.

7.2 Data Requirements

· Full textual production of each author over the panel.
· Engagement metadata: who engaged, when, with which content.
· Community membership proxies for engagers (topic, other interactions, declared affiliations where public).
· Platform documentation of any policy changes within the panel window.

7.3 Attrition

Author attrition is anticipated. We preregister:

· inverse-probability weighting for dropout;
· sensitivity analysis comparing attrited and retained authors on baseline Z and \widetilde A.

7.4 Statistical Power

Simulation-based power analysis is preregistered. We require power \geq 0.8 for detecting an effect size of d=0.3 on the primary contrast G_2>G_1, under a design with within-author fixed effects and the anticipated panel size.

If power is insufficient, the panel is extended or the primary contrast is restricted to a higher-variance subsample.

---

8. Estimation

8.1 Primary Model

\Delta F'_{i}=\alpha+\beta\,\mathbf 1\{G_2\}+\gamma\,\Delta Q_i+\delta\,\mathbf 1\{G_1\}+\eta X_i+\epsilon_i,

where the reference category is G_0.

The coefficient of interest is \beta-\delta, the differential effect of audience mutation beyond distribution increase.

8.2 Within-Author Specification

To control for time-invariant author characteristics, we estimate a within-author variant across the full panel:

Y_{it}=\alpha_i+\lambda_t+\beta_1\,\text{Mut}_{it}+\beta_2\,Q_{it}+\beta_3\,(\text{Mut}_{it}\times Q_{it})+\gamma X_{it}+\epsilon_{it},

where \text{Mut}_{it} is a binary indicator for audience mutation in the relevant window.

8.3 Mediation Check

The Recursive Mirror predicts that the effect of Q on Z' operates through \widetilde A, not directly:

Q\to\widetilde A\to Z'.

We test this with temporal mediation analysis:

\widetilde A_{t+1}=\alpha_i+\eta Q_{t+1}+\omega Z_t+\epsilon,

Z_{t+2}=\alpha_i+\theta\widetilde A_{t+1}+\rho Z_t+\nu.

If \theta is significant and \eta is significant, the loop is compatible. If \theta\approx 0, the loop is not detected.

8.4 Robustness

Preregistered robustness checks:

· alternative JSD thresholds for G_2;
· alternative windows for t, t+1, t+2;
· alternative measures of \phi (sentence embeddings, topic models, hand-coded features);
· alternative measures of \Psi (community embeddings, ideological proxies, engagement-history features);
· exclusion of high-profile authors whose audience composition is dominated by exogenous factors.

---

9. Ethics and Dual Use

9.1 The Dual-Use Problem

The Recursive Mirror, if true, describes a mechanism by which an author or a coordinated group could deliberately transform an audience in order to transform future production — their own or others'. This is not a neutral finding.

We distinguish:

· Explanation: understanding whether and how audience transformation feeds back into production.
· Optimization: providing an operational method for deliberately reshaping audiences to reshape production.

This paper pursues the first. It does not provide:

· targeting procedures;
· manipulation strategies;
· recommendations for maximizing audience mutation;
· methods for identifying vulnerable authors.

9.2 Transparency

We argue, as in the first component, that transparency is preferable to secrecy. The mechanism, if it exists, is already operating at scale. Documenting it in the scientific literature is a precondition for public debate and regulatory attention.

9.3 Data Protection

Authors in the panel are public figures only insofar as their production is public. We:

· do not collect private messages;
· do not infer individual-level sensitive attributes;
· aggregate audience characteristics at community level, never individual;
· preregister anonymization of any illustrative examples.

---

10. Limitations

Limitation 1. Even under Designs A or B, the quasi-experimental identification of audience mutation is not perfect. Residual confounding by unobserved content characteristics remains possible.

Limitation 2. \widetilde A is a proxy for reactive audience, not total audience. Effects estimated on reactive audiences may not generalize to silent exposure.

Limitation 3. Production change \Delta Z' is measured on the observable linguistic vector Z. Changes in production that are not captured by Z — thematic subtleties, political alignments, personal voice — may escape measurement.

Limitation 4. The mechanism, if it operates, may operate on a longer horizon than the panel covers. Absence of detection at 12–24 months does not imply absence of effect.

Limitation 5. The framework is single-platform per panel. Cross-platform adaptation, if it exists, is not addressed.

Limitation 6. Authorial intent is unobserved. The Recursive Mirror does not require conscious adaptation; it is compatible with both deliberate strategy and unconscious drift. The framework does not distinguish them.

Limitation 7. The bilinear form F=\langle\phi,\Psi\rangle is one possible formalization of relational fitness. Others exist. The framework tests this one, not the concept in general.

---

11. Relation to Component One

11.1 Continuity

Component One and Component Two are designed to be read together:

 Component One Component Two
Segment Z\to M\to Q\to\widetilde A \widetilde A\to Z'
Horizon 3–6 months 12–24 months
Design Within-author FE + matched control Panel + quasi-experimental
Primary question Does structure predict propagation? Does propagation transform production?
Falsifications F1–F6 (upstream) F1–F6 (downstream)

The two components share the linguistic vector Z, the distribution proxy Q, the audience proxy \widetilde A, and the bilinear fitness form F.

11.2 What Can Be Concluded from Each

Component One can conclude:

· whether certain linguistic configurations are associated with distinctive responses and distribution;
· whether those associations are robust to author and time fixed effects;
· whether the proxies Q and \widetilde A behave as hypothesized.

Component Two can conclude:

· whether audience mutation is associated with subsequent production change, beyond what distribution alone explains;
· whether that change aligns with the mutated audience;
· whether the alignment is detectable under quasi-experimental conditions.

Neither component alone establishes the Recursive Mirror. Together, they provide the strongest currently feasible test.

---

12. Conclusion

The Recursive Mirror proposes that in algorithmically mediated environments, the propagation of content transforms the audience, and the transformed audience transforms the producer. This paper has specified the longitudinal framework necessary to test the downstream half of this loop.

The central design compares authors whose distribution increased with and without simultaneous audience mutation. The prediction is that only the latter group exhibits systematic production change aligned with the new audience. If confirmed, this distinguishes virality — replication through performance — from recursive mirroring — production through audience transformation.

The framework is deliberately disciplined:

· the latent construct F is separated from its observable manifestations;
· the quasi-experimental designs are preregistered;
· the null results are as theoretically meaningful as the positive ones;
· the limits are stated before the results are known.

The loop, if it exists, is a property of the coupled author–text–audience–algorithm system, not of any of its components alone. Documenting it is not the same as exploiting it.

What we propose here is the instrument to determine whether the loop is real.

